A common way to enroll the TPM, is to bind to one of its PCR banks. These banks
store encrypted system information of various kind, most notably the Secure Boot
status in PCR7. The TPM will compare current values against the ones in the PCR
banks and only unlock a secret when these values match. However, values may
often change due to actions that are not security related - especially in a dual
boot environment.

For that reason, instead of binding directly to PCR banks, we will bind to a
"PCR policy". A policy refers to the expected state of multiple PCR banks at
specific phases of the boot process. It will create a digest, a hash of all
expected PCR values at one of these specific stages, sign it with a private key,
and package it with the UKI. Then, when an encrypted volume with an enrolled
TPM2 secret is encountered during the boot process, the current state of the PCR
banks will be matched against all available digests in the UKI. If one matches
and the digest can be verified with the public key also packaged in the UKI, the
volume will be unlocked.

